-{"slug":"article67","file":"article67.html","en":{"title":"#67 - Rituals that bring people together: powwow, sweat lodge and living traditions","html":"<article class=\"article-ecoute\">\n<h1>#67 — Rituals that bring people together: powwow, sweat lodge and living traditions</h1>\n\n<div class=\"meta\">\n    Série <em>Peuples Autochtones du Québec</em> — Article #67<br>\n    Groupe Facebook : <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/groups/1451283625021958\" target=\"_blank\">Les Premières Nations au Québec</a>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"lead\">\n    « Un pow-wow n'est pas un spectacle. Une tente à sudation n'est pas un spa. Ces cérémonies sont des actes de résistance — la preuve vivante que malgré 400 ans de colonisation, les cultures autochtones du Québec pulsent encore. »\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"clearfix\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/040588c13_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Pow-wow traditionnel des Premières Nations\" class=\"img-float-right\">\n    <p>Indigenous ceremonies have long been banned in Canada. The Indian Act of 1885 criminalized the potlatch and several ceremonial dances. The powwow was tolerated only because it could be presented as \"entertainment\" to the settlers.</p>\n    <p>Today, these bans have been lifted since 1951. And the ceremonies live on — more than ever. They are the beating heart of the indigenous cultural renaissance in Quebec.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>The powwow — much more than a festival</h2>\n\n<p>The powwow is a pan-Indigenous ceremonial gathering — it crosses borders between nations. Cree, Mohawk, Algonquin and Innu dancers meet there, in the same space of sharing and celebration.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The three phases of a powwow:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>The Grand Entrance</strong> — the dancers enter in procession, complete regalia, carrying the flags of the nations. Solemn, spiritually charged moment.</li>\n    <li><strong>Competitive dances</strong> — each style of dance (fancy, jingle, grass, traditional) has its codes, its meanings, its age categories. It's not a show: it's a danced prayer.</li>\n    <li><strong>The community meal</strong> — bannock, corn soup, smoked meat. The meal is as important as the dance: it nourishes the bond between people.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/040588c13_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Danseurs au pow-wow\" class=\"img-center\">\n<p class=\"img-caption\">Regalia (ceremonial costumes) are sewn by hand, often by the family — every feather, every bead has a meaning.</p>\n\n<h2>The sweat lodge—spiritual technology</h2>\n\n<div class=\"clearfix\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/040588c13_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Tente à sudation autochtone\" class=\"img-float-right\">\n    <p>The sweat tent (<em>lodge sweatshirt</em>, Or <em>madoodiswan</em> in Ojibwe) is a dome structure covered with skins or blankets, heated by hot stones sprinkled with water. There are several of us entering, in complete darkness, the intense heat.</p>\n    <p>It's not a sauna. It's a <strong>purification ceremony</strong> — physical, mental and spiritual. Participants pray, sing, share intentions. Extreme heat pushes you to the limits — and beyond that, to clarity.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"highlight\">\n    <strong>Important note:</strong> La tente à sudation ne se fait pas en touriste. Elle se fait sous la guidance d'un aîné certifié par sa communauté. Des \"facilitateurs\" non autochtones qui organisent des sweat lodges commerciales sans formation adéquate ont causé des accidents mortels (Arizona, 2009). Respectez ce protocole : approchez toujours par les communautés officielles.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Powwows in Quebec — where and when?</h2>\n\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Kahnawake Powwow</strong> — July, Mohawks of Kahnawake. One of the largest in Quebec.</li>\n    <li><strong>Wendake Powwow</strong> — July, Hurons-Wendat. Accessible from Quebec.</li>\n    <li><strong>Innu Nikamu Festival</strong> — Maliotenam (Côte-Nord) — unique indigenous music festival.</li>\n    <li><strong>Wôlinak Powwow</strong> — Abenakis, Bécancour — along the river.</li>\n    <li><strong>Indigenous Peoples Week</strong> — Montreal, June — events throughout the city.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"quote\">\n    « Quand je mets ma regalia, je ne deviens pas un personnage. Je deviens moi-même — le moi que mes ancêtres auraient voulu que je sois. »<br>\n    — <em>Danseuse Jingle Dress, pow-wow de Kahnawake, 2021</em>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"cta\">\n    <p>🌿 <strong>Tip for the visitor</strong></p>\n    <p>At the powwow: ask before photographing, do not touch the regalia without permission, do not cross the arena during a dance. These simple rules are the minimum of respect. In return, you will live one of the most profound cultural experiences that Quebec offers.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"hashtags\">\n    #PowWow #PremièresNations #AutochtonesQuébec #TenteÀSudation #SweetLodge #KahnawakePowWow #WendakePowWow #InnuNikamu #CérémoniesAutochtones #ReconciliationCanada\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"footer-sources\">\n    <strong>Sources:</strong> Patrimoine culturel du Québec — pow-wow.com — Tourisme autochtone Québec — Loi sur les Indiens, histoire des interdictions 1885-1951 — Archives nationales du Canada\n</div>\n\n</article>\n\n<div class=\"nav-art\">\n    <a href=\"article66.html\">← #66 Gastronomie</a>\n    <a href=\"article68.html\">#68 Figures marquantes →</a>\n</div>"},"es":{"title":"#67 - Rituales que unen a las personas: powwow, sauna y tradiciones vivas","html":"<article class=\"article-ecoute\">\n<h1>#67 — Rituales que unen a las personas: powwow, sauna y tradiciones vivas</h1>\n\n<div class=\"meta\">\n    Série <em>Peuples Autochtones du Québec</em> — Article #67<br>\n    Groupe Facebook : <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/groups/1451283625021958\" target=\"_blank\">Les Premières Nations au Québec</a>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"lead\">\n    « Un pow-wow n'est pas un spectacle. Une tente à sudation n'est pas un spa. Ces cérémonies sont des actes de résistance — la preuve vivante que malgré 400 ans de colonisation, les cultures autochtones du Québec pulsent encore. »\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"clearfix\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/040588c13_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Pow-wow traditionnel des Premières Nations\" class=\"img-float-right\">\n    <p>Las ceremonias indígenas están prohibidas desde hace mucho tiempo en Canadá. La Ley India de 1885 criminalizó el potlatch y varias danzas ceremoniales. El powwow fue tolerado sólo porque podía presentarse como \"entretenimiento\" a los colonos.</p>\n    <p>Hoy en día, estas prohibiciones se han levantado desde 1951. Y las ceremonias siguen vivas, más que nunca. Son el corazón palpitante del renacimiento cultural indígena en Quebec.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>El powwow: mucho más que un festival</h2>\n\n<p>El powwow es una reunión ceremonial panindígena: cruza fronteras entre naciones. Allí se reúnen bailarines Cree, Mohawk, Algonquin e Innu, en un mismo espacio de intercambio y celebración.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Las tres fases de un powwow:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>La gran entrada</strong> — los bailarines entran en procesión, con todas sus insignias y portando las banderas de las naciones. Momento solemne y cargado de espiritualidad.</li>\n    <li><strong>bailes competitivos</strong> — cada estilo de danza (elegante, jingle, hierba, tradicional) tiene sus códigos, sus significados, sus categorías de edad. No es un espectáculo: es una oración danzada.</li>\n    <li><strong>la comida comunitaria</strong> — bannock, sopa de maíz, carne ahumada. La comida es tan importante como el baile: nutre el vínculo entre las personas.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/040588c13_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Danseurs au pow-wow\" class=\"img-center\">\n<p class=\"img-caption\">Los regalia (trajes ceremoniales) se cosen a mano, a menudo por la familia: cada pluma, cada cuenta tiene un significado.</p>\n\n<h2>La cabaña de sudor: tecnología espiritual</h2>\n\n<div class=\"clearfix\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/040588c13_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Tente à sudation autochtone\" class=\"img-float-right\">\n    <p>La tienda de sudor (<em>sudadera con capucha</em>, O <em>madoodiswan</em> en Ojibwe) es una estructura de cúpula cubierta con pieles o mantas, calentada con piedras calientes rociadas con agua. Somos varios adentrándonos, en completa oscuridad, en el intenso calor.</p>\n    <p>No es una sauna. es un <strong>ceremonia de purificación</strong> – físico, mental y espiritual. Los participantes rezan, cantan, comparten intenciones. El calor extremo te lleva al límite y, más allá de eso, a la claridad.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"highlight\">\n    <strong>Nota importante:</strong> La tente à sudation ne se fait pas en touriste. Elle se fait sous la guidance d'un aîné certifié par sa communauté. Des \"facilitateurs\" non autochtones qui organisent des sweat lodges commerciales sans formation adéquate ont causé des accidents mortels (Arizona, 2009). Respectez ce protocole : approchez toujours par les communautés officielles.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Powwows en Quebec: ¿dónde y cuándo?</h2>\n\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Powwow de Kahnawake</strong> — Julio, Mohawks de Kahnawake. Uno de los más grandes de Quebec.</li>\n    <li><strong>Wendake Powwow</strong> — Julio, Hurones-Wendat. Accesible desde Quebec.</li>\n    <li><strong>Festival Innu Nikamu</strong> — Maliotenam (Costa Norte): festival de música indígena único.</li>\n    <li><strong>Powwow de Wôlinak</strong> — Abenakis, Bécancour — a lo largo del río.</li>\n    <li><strong>Semana de los Pueblos Indígenas</strong> — Montreal, junio — eventos en toda la ciudad.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"quote\">\n    « Quand je mets ma regalia, je ne deviens pas un personnage. Je deviens moi-même — le moi que mes ancêtres auraient voulu que je sois. »<br>\n    — <em>Danseuse Jingle Dress, pow-wow de Kahnawake, 2021</em>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"cta\">\n    <p>🌿 <strong>Consejo para el visitante</strong></p>\n    <p>En el powwow: pregunta antes de fotografiar, no toques las insignias sin permiso, no cruces la arena durante un baile. Estas simples reglas son el mínimo de respeto. A cambio, vivirá una de las experiencias culturales más profundas que ofrece Quebec.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"hashtags\">\n    #PowWow #PremièresNations #AutochtonesQuébec #TenteÀSudation #SweetLodge #KahnawakePowWow #WendakePowWow #InnuNikamu #CérémoniesAutochtones #ReconciliationCanada\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"footer-sources\">\n    <strong>Fuentes:</strong> Patrimoine culturel du Québec — pow-wow.com — Tourisme autochtone Québec — Loi sur les Indiens, histoire des interdictions 1885-1951 — Archives nationales du Canada\n</div>\n\n</article>\n\n<div class=\"nav-art\">\n    <a href=\"article66.html\">← #66 Gastronomie</a>\n    <a href=\"article68.html\">#68 Figures marquantes →</a>\n</div>"}}
+{"slug":"article67","file":"article67.html","en":{"title":"#67 - Rituals that bring people together: powwow, sweat lodge and living traditions","html":"<article class=\"article-ecoute\">\n<h1>#67 — Rituals that bring people together: powwow, sweat lodge and living traditions</h1>\n\n<div class=\"meta\">\n    Série <em>Peuples Autochtones du Québec</em> — Article #67<br>\n    Groupe Facebook : <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/groups/1451283625021958\" target=\"_blank\">Les Premières Nations au Québec</a>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"lead\">\n    « Un pow-wow n'est pas un spectacle. Une tente à sudation n'est pas un spa. Ces cérémonies sont des actes de résistance — la preuve vivante que malgré 400 ans de colonisation, les cultures autochtones du Québec pulsent encore. »\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"clearfix\">\n    <img src=\"assets/images/c1af84dd7_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Pow-wow traditionnel des Premières Nations\" class=\"img-float-right\">\n    <p>Indigenous ceremonies have long been banned in Canada. The Indian Act of 1885 criminalized the potlatch and several ceremonial dances. The powwow was tolerated only because it could be presented as \"entertainment\" to the settlers.</p>\n    <p>Today, these bans have been lifted since 1951. And the ceremonies live on — more than ever. They are the beating heart of the indigenous cultural renaissance in Quebec.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>The powwow — much more than a festival</h2>\n\n<p>The powwow is a pan-Indigenous ceremonial gathering — it crosses borders between nations. Cree, Mohawk, Algonquin and Innu dancers meet there, in the same space of sharing and celebration.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The three phases of a powwow:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>The Grand Entrance</strong> — the dancers enter in procession, complete regalia, carrying the flags of the nations. Solemn, spiritually charged moment.</li>\n    <li><strong>Competitive dances</strong> — each style of dance (fancy, jingle, grass, traditional) has its codes, its meanings, its age categories. It's not a show: it's a danced prayer.</li>\n    <li><strong>The community meal</strong> — bannock, corn soup, smoked meat. The meal is as important as the dance: it nourishes the bond between people.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<img src=\"assets/images/c1af84dd7_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Danseurs au pow-wow\" class=\"img-center\">\n<p class=\"img-caption\">Regalia (ceremonial costumes) are sewn by hand, often by the family — every feather, every bead has a meaning.</p>\n\n<h2>The sweat lodge—spiritual technology</h2>\n\n<div class=\"clearfix\">\n    <img src=\"assets/images/c1af84dd7_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Tente à sudation autochtone\" class=\"img-float-right\">\n    <p>The sweat tent (<em>lodge sweatshirt</em>, Or <em>madoodiswan</em> in Ojibwe) is a dome structure covered with skins or blankets, heated by hot stones sprinkled with water. There are several of us entering, in complete darkness, the intense heat.</p>\n    <p>It's not a sauna. It's a <strong>purification ceremony</strong> — physical, mental and spiritual. Participants pray, sing, share intentions. Extreme heat pushes you to the limits — and beyond that, to clarity.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"highlight\">\n    <strong>Important note:</strong> La tente à sudation ne se fait pas en touriste. Elle se fait sous la guidance d'un aîné certifié par sa communauté. Des \"facilitateurs\" non autochtones qui organisent des sweat lodges commerciales sans formation adéquate ont causé des accidents mortels (Arizona, 2009). Respectez ce protocole : approchez toujours par les communautés officielles.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Powwows in Quebec — where and when?</h2>\n\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Kahnawake Powwow</strong> — July, Mohawks of Kahnawake. One of the largest in Quebec.</li>\n    <li><strong>Wendake Powwow</strong> — July, Hurons-Wendat. Accessible from Quebec.</li>\n    <li><strong>Innu Nikamu Festival</strong> — Maliotenam (Côte-Nord) — unique indigenous music festival.</li>\n    <li><strong>Wôlinak Powwow</strong> — Abenakis, Bécancour — along the river.</li>\n    <li><strong>Indigenous Peoples Week</strong> — Montreal, June — events throughout the city.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"quote\">\n    « Quand je mets ma regalia, je ne deviens pas un personnage. Je deviens moi-même — le moi que mes ancêtres auraient voulu que je sois. »<br>\n    — <em>Danseuse Jingle Dress, pow-wow de Kahnawake, 2021</em>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"cta\">\n    <p>🌿 <strong>Tip for the visitor</strong></p>\n    <p>At the powwow: ask before photographing, do not touch the regalia without permission, do not cross the arena during a dance. These simple rules are the minimum of respect. In return, you will live one of the most profound cultural experiences that Quebec offers.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"hashtags\">\n    #PowWow #PremièresNations #AutochtonesQuébec #TenteÀSudation #SweetLodge #KahnawakePowWow #WendakePowWow #InnuNikamu #CérémoniesAutochtones #ReconciliationCanada\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"footer-sources\">\n    <strong>Sources:</strong> Patrimoine culturel du Québec — pow-wow.com — Tourisme autochtone Québec — Loi sur les Indiens, histoire des interdictions 1885-1951 — Archives nationales du Canada\n</div>\n\n</article>\n\n<div class=\"nav-art\">\n    <a href=\"article66.html\">← #66 Gastronomie</a>\n    <a href=\"article68.html\">#68 Figures marquantes →</a>\n</div>"},"es":{"title":"#67 - Rituales que unen a las personas: powwow, sauna y tradiciones vivas","html":"<article class=\"article-ecoute\">\n<h1>#67 — Rituales que unen a las personas: powwow, sauna y tradiciones vivas</h1>\n\n<div class=\"meta\">\n    Série <em>Peuples Autochtones du Québec</em> — Article #67<br>\n    Groupe Facebook : <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/groups/1451283625021958\" target=\"_blank\">Les Premières Nations au Québec</a>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"lead\">\n    « Un pow-wow n'est pas un spectacle. Une tente à sudation n'est pas un spa. Ces cérémonies sont des actes de résistance — la preuve viv