-{"slug":"article68","file":"article68.html","en":{"title":"#68 - Faces of a resistance: portraits of significant figures from the First Nations of Quebec","html":"<article class=\"article-ecoute\">\n<h1>#68 — Faces of a resistance: portraits of significant figures from the First Nations of Quebec</h1>\n\n<div class=\"meta\">\n    Série <em>Peuples Autochtones du Québec</em> — Article #68<br>\n    Groupe Facebook : <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/groups/1451283625021958\" target=\"_blank\">Les Premières Nations au Québec</a>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"lead\">\n    « L'histoire des Premières Nations du Québec n'est pas seulement une histoire de souffrance — c'est une histoire de personnes extraordinaires qui ont résisté, créé, plaidé, construit. Il est temps de les nommer. »\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"clearfix\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/650dae6de_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Figures marquantes des Premières Nations du Québec\" class=\"img-float-right\">\n    <p>Quebec school textbooks have long told the history of the province through the eyes of the settlers. The indigenous figures who appeared there were rarely subjects - rather settings of the European adventure.</p>\n    <p>It is time to honor these men and women who shaped this territory long before Champlain, and who continue to do so today.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Historical figures</h2>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🦅 Donnacona — Chief Stadaconé (Huron-Iroquois, ~1500-1539)</h3>\n    <p>Chief of the village of Stadaconé (today Quebec), Donnacona was one of the first indigenous interlocutors of Jacques Cartier during the travels of 1534-1535. He tried to establish balanced diplomatic relations - before being kidnapped by Cartier and taken to France, where he died in 1539. His tragedy heralds that of an entire people.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🌹 Kateri Tekakwitha — Saint Mohawk (1656-1680)</h3>\n    <p>Born in Ossernenon (present-day New York) to an Algonquin mother and a Mohawk father, Kateri was baptized in Kahnawake and lived the rest of her short life in the Christian faith, while maintaining her Mohawk traditions. Canonized by Pope Benedict XVI in 2012 — the first indigenous saint in North America. Figure of cultural resistance and spiritual synthesis.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🐻 Membertou — Great Micmac chief (~1510-1611)</h3>\n    <p>Supreme leader of the Micmacs of Nova Scotia and Quebec, Membertou was the first Aboriginal person baptized in New France (1610). An outstanding diplomat, he maintained balanced relations with the French while preserving the autonomy of his people. He died at over 100, respected on both sides.</p>\n</div>\n\n<img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/650dae6de_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Leaders autochtones du Québec\" class=\"img-center\">\n<p class=\"img-caption\">Centuries of Indigenous leadership — often erased from official history.</p>\n\n<h2>Contemporary figures</h2>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>✊ Élijah Harper — Manitoba Cree (1949-2013)</h3>\n    <p>Although Manitoban, Élijah Harper is a pan-Canadian figure. In 1990, holding an eagle feather, he simply said \"No\" to the Meech Lake Accord in the Manitoba legislature — single-handedly blocking a constitutional agreement that ignored Indigenous rights. A historic gesture that changed the political course of Canada.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🎵 Florent Vollant — Innu musician (born in 1959)</h3>\n    <p>Co-founder of the Kashtin duo with Claude McKenzie, Florent Vollant is the most famous Innu musician in the world. His songs in Innu-aimun have sold hundreds of thousands of copies. He is also an active activist for the revitalization of the Innu language and territorial rights of the North Shore.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>⚖️ Romeo Saganash — Cri de Waswanipi (born in 1962)</h3>\n    <p>Indian residential school survivor, lawyer, federal MP (NDP, 2011-2019), Romeo Saganash defended indigenous rights at the UN, contributed to the drafting of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, and represented the Wampum Belt on the international stage.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🌊 Manon Barbeau — Director, Atikamekw ally</h3>\n    <p>Founder of Wapikoni Mobile — a mobile studio that travels through Indigenous communities in Quebec to train young Indigenous filmmakers. More than 1,200 short films produced in 15 countries. A cultural revolution born from a caravan and a camera.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quote\">\n    « On ne nous a pas demandé si on voulait être colonisés. On ne nous demandera pas si on veut se décoloniser. On va le faire. »<br>\n    — <em>Romeo Saganash, discours à l'ONU, 2007</em>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"cta\">\n    <p>🌿 <strong>To go further</strong></p>\n    <p>Watch the documentary <strong>“We are your allies”</strong> on young Wapikoni filmmakers — available on wapikoni.tv — a direct window into today's Indigenous generation.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"hashtags\">\n    #PremièresNations #FiguresAutochtones #ElijahHarper #FlorentVollant #KateriTekakwitha #RomeoSaganash #WapikoniMobile #Donnacona #CrisQuébec #Mohawks #ReconciliationCanada\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"footer-sources\">\n    <strong>Sources:</strong> Bibliothèque et Archives Canada — CVR du Canada — Wapikoni.tv — Dictionnaire biographique du Canada — Saganash, R. (2019). Mémoires parlementaires\n</div>\n\n</article>\n\n<div class=\"nav-art\">\n    <a href=\"article67.html\">← #67 Rituels</a>\n    <a href=\"article69.html\">#69 Tourisme responsable →</a>\n</div>"},"es":{"title":"#68 - Rostros de una resistencia: retratos de figuras significativas de las Primeras Naciones de Quebec","html":"<article class=\"article-ecoute\">\n<h1>#68 — Rostros de una resistencia: retratos de figuras significativas de las Primeras Naciones de Quebec</h1>\n\n<div class=\"meta\">\n    Série <em>Peuples Autochtones du Québec</em> — Article #68<br>\n    Groupe Facebook : <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/groups/1451283625021958\" target=\"_blank\">Les Premières Nations au Québec</a>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"lead\">\n    « L'histoire des Premières Nations du Québec n'est pas seulement une histoire de souffrance — c'est une histoire de personnes extraordinaires qui ont résisté, créé, plaidé, construit. Il est temps de les nommer. »\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"clearfix\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/650dae6de_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Figures marquantes des Premières Nations du Québec\" class=\"img-float-right\">\n    <p>Los libros de texto escolares de Quebec cuentan desde hace mucho tiempo la historia de la provincia a través de los ojos de los colonos. Las figuras indígenas que aparecían allí rara vez eran sujetos, más bien escenarios de la aventura europea.</p>\n    <p>Es hora de honrar a estos hombres y mujeres que dieron forma a este territorio mucho antes que Champlain, y que continúan haciéndolo hoy.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Personajes historicos</h2>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🦅 Donnacona — Jefe Stadaconé (Huron-Iroquois, ~1500-1539)</h3>\n    <p>Jefe del pueblo de Stadaconé (hoy Quebec), Donnacona fue uno de los primeros interlocutores indígenas de Jacques Cartier durante los viajes de 1534-1535. Intentó establecer relaciones diplomáticas equilibradas, antes de ser secuestrado por Cartier y llevado a Francia, donde murió en 1539. Su tragedia presagia la de todo un pueblo.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🌹 Kateri Tekakwitha - Santa Mohawk (1656-1680)</h3>\n    <p>Nacida en Ossernenon (actual Nueva York) de madre algonquina y padre mohawk, Kateri fue bautizada en Kahnawake y vivió el resto de su corta vida en la fe cristiana, manteniendo sus tradiciones mohawk. Canonizado por el Papa Benedicto XVI en 2012, el primer santo indígena de América del Norte. Figura de resistencia cultural y síntesis espiritual.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🐻 Membertou - Gran jefe Micmac (~1510-1611)</h3>\n    <p>Líder supremo de los Micmacs de Nueva Escocia y Quebec, Membertou fue el primer aborigen bautizado en Nueva Francia (1610). Destacado diplomático, mantuvo relaciones equilibradas con los franceses preservando al mismo tiempo la autonomía de su pueblo. Murió con más de 100 años, respetado por ambos lados.</p>\n</div>\n\n<img src=\"https://media.base44.com/images/public/69f23c5b09417d29099136be/650dae6de_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Leaders autochtones du Québec\" class=\"img-center\">\n<p class=\"img-caption\">Siglos de liderazgo indígena, a menudo borrados de la historia oficial.</p>\n\n<h2>Figuras contemporáneas</h2>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>✊ Élijah Harper — Cree de Manitoba (1949-2013)</h3>\n    <p>Aunque Manitoban, Élijah Harper es una figura pancanadiense. En 1990, sosteniendo una pluma de águila, simplemente dijo \"No\" al Acuerdo del Lago Meech en la legislatura de Manitoba, bloqueando por sí solo un acuerdo constitucional que ignoraba los derechos indígenas. Un gesto histórico que cambió el rumbo político de Canadá.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🎵 Florent Vollant - Músico innu (nacido en 1959)</h3>\n    <p>Florent Vollant, cofundador del dúo Kashtin con Claude McKenzie, es el músico innu más famoso del mundo. Sus canciones en Innu-aimun han vendido cientos de miles de copias. También es un activista activo por la revitalización de la lengua innu y los derechos territoriales de la costa norte.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>⚖️ Romeo Saganash — Cri de Waswanipi (nacido en 1962)</h3>\n    <p>Romeo Saganash, superviviente de un internado indio, abogado, diputado federal (NDP, 2011-2019), defendió los derechos indígenas en la ONU, contribuyó a la redacción de la Declaración de las Naciones Unidas sobre los derechos de los pueblos indígenas y representó al Wampum Belt en la escena internacional.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🌊 Manon Barbeau - Directora, aliado de Atikamekw</h3>\n    <p>Fundador de Wapikoni Mobile, un estudio móvil que viaja a través de comunidades indígenas de Quebec para formar a jóvenes cineastas indígenas. Más de 1.200 cortometrajes producidos en 15 países. Una revolución cultural nacida de una caravana y una cámara.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quote\">\n    « On ne nous a pas demandé si on voulait être colonisés. On ne nous demandera pas si on veut se décoloniser. On va le faire. »<br>\n    — <em>Romeo Saganash, discours à l'ONU, 2007</em>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"cta\">\n    <p>🌿 <strong>para ir más lejos</strong></p>\n    <p>Mira el documental <strong>“Somos tus aliados”</strong> sobre jóvenes cineastas wapikoni, disponible en wapikoni.tv, una ventana directa a la generación indígena actual.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"hashtags\">\n    #PremièresNations #FiguresAutochtones #ElijahHarper #FlorentVollant #KateriTekakwitha #RomeoSaganash #WapikoniMobile #Donnacona #CrisQuébec #Mohawks #ReconciliationCanada\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"footer-sources\">\n    <strong>Fuentes:</strong> Bibliothèque et Archives Canada — CVR du Canada — Wapikoni.tv — Dictionnaire biographique du Canada — Saganash, R. (2019). Mémoires parlementaires\n</div>\n\n</article>\n\n<div class=\"nav-art\">\n    <a href=\"article67.html\">← #67 Rituels</a>\n    <a href=\"article69.html\">#69 Tourisme responsable →</a>\n</div>"}}
+{"slug":"article68","file":"article68.html","en":{"title":"#68 - Faces of a resistance: portraits of significant figures from the First Nations of Quebec","html":"<article class=\"article-ecoute\">\n<h1>#68 — Faces of a resistance: portraits of significant figures from the First Nations of Quebec</h1>\n\n<div class=\"meta\">\n    Série <em>Peuples Autochtones du Québec</em> — Article #68<br>\n    Groupe Facebook : <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/groups/1451283625021958\" target=\"_blank\">Les Premières Nations au Québec</a>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"lead\">\n    « L'histoire des Premières Nations du Québec n'est pas seulement une histoire de souffrance — c'est une histoire de personnes extraordinaires qui ont résisté, créé, plaidé, construit. Il est temps de les nommer. »\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"clearfix\">\n    <img src=\"assets/images/9af16d09b_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Figures marquantes des Premières Nations du Québec\" class=\"img-float-right\">\n    <p>Quebec school textbooks have long told the history of the province through the eyes of the settlers. The indigenous figures who appeared there were rarely subjects - rather settings of the European adventure.</p>\n    <p>It is time to honor these men and women who shaped this territory long before Champlain, and who continue to do so today.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Historical figures</h2>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🦅 Donnacona — Chief Stadaconé (Huron-Iroquois, ~1500-1539)</h3>\n    <p>Chief of the village of Stadaconé (today Quebec), Donnacona was one of the first indigenous interlocutors of Jacques Cartier during the travels of 1534-1535. He tried to establish balanced diplomatic relations - before being kidnapped by Cartier and taken to France, where he died in 1539. His tragedy heralds that of an entire people.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🌹 Kateri Tekakwitha — Saint Mohawk (1656-1680)</h3>\n    <p>Born in Ossernenon (present-day New York) to an Algonquin mother and a Mohawk father, Kateri was baptized in Kahnawake and lived the rest of her short life in the Christian faith, while maintaining her Mohawk traditions. Canonized by Pope Benedict XVI in 2012 — the first indigenous saint in North America. Figure of cultural resistance and spiritual synthesis.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🐻 Membertou — Great Micmac chief (~1510-1611)</h3>\n    <p>Supreme leader of the Micmacs of Nova Scotia and Quebec, Membertou was the first Aboriginal person baptized in New France (1610). An outstanding diplomat, he maintained balanced relations with the French while preserving the autonomy of his people. He died at over 100, respected on both sides.</p>\n</div>\n\n<img src=\"assets/images/9af16d09b_generated_image.png\" alt=\"Leaders autochtones du Québec\" class=\"img-center\">\n<p class=\"img-caption\">Centuries of Indigenous leadership — often erased from official history.</p>\n\n<h2>Contemporary figures</h2>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>✊ Élijah Harper — Manitoba Cree (1949-2013)</h3>\n    <p>Although Manitoban, Élijah Harper is a pan-Canadian figure. In 1990, holding an eagle feather, he simply said \"No\" to the Meech Lake Accord in the Manitoba legislature — single-handedly blocking a constitutional agreement that ignored Indigenous rights. A historic gesture that changed the political course of Canada.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🎵 Florent Vollant — Innu musician (born in 1959)</h3>\n    <p>Co-founder of the Kashtin duo with Claude McKenzie, Florent Vollant is the most famous Innu musician in the world. His songs in Innu-aimun have sold hundreds of thousands of copies. He is also an active activist for the revitalization of the Innu language and territorial rights of the North Shore.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>⚖️ Romeo Saganash — Cri de Waswanipi (born in 1962)</h3>\n    <p>Indian residential school survivor, lawyer, federal MP (NDP, 2011-2019), Romeo Saganash defended indigenous rights at the UN, contributed to the drafting of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, and represented the Wampum Belt on the international stage.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"portrait\">\n    <h3>🌊 Manon Barbeau — Director, Atikamekw ally</h3>\n    <p>Founder of Wapikoni Mobile — a mobile studio that travels through Indigenous communities in Quebec to train young Indigenous filmmakers. More than 1,200 short films produced in 15 countries. A cultural revolution born from a caravan and a camera.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quote\">\n    « On ne nous a pas demandé si on voulait être colonisés. On ne nous demandera pas si on veut se décoloniser. On va le faire. »<br>\n    — <em>Romeo Saganash, discours à l'ONU, 2007</em>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"cta\">\n    <p>🌿 <strong>To go further</strong></p>\n    <p>Watch the documentary <strong>“We are your allies”</strong> on young Wapikoni filmmakers — available on wapikoni.tv — a direct window into today's Indigenous generation.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"hashtags\">\n    #PremièresNations #FiguresAutochtones #ElijahHarper #FlorentVollant #KateriTekakwitha #RomeoSaganash #WapikoniMobile #Donnacona #CrisQuébec #Mohawks #ReconciliationCanada\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"footer-sources\">\n    <strong>Sources:</strong> Bibliothèque et Archives Canada — CVR du Canada — Wapikoni.tv — Dictionnaire biographique du Canada — Saganash, R. (2019). Mémoires parlementaires\n</div>\n\n</article>\n\n<div class=\"nav-art\">\n    <a href=\"article67.html\">← #67 Rituels</a>\n    <a href=\"article69.html\">#69 Tourisme responsable →</a>\n</div>"},"es":{"title":"#68 - Rostros de una resistencia: retratos de figuras significativas de las Primeras Naciones de Quebec","html":"<article class=\"article-ecoute\">\n<h1>#68 — Rostros de una resistencia: retratos de figuras significativas de las Primeras Naciones de Quebec</h1>\n\n<div class=\"meta\">\n    Série <em>Peuples Autochtones du Québec</em> — Article #68<br>\n    Groupe Face